[20/200] Running: Compare data parallelism and pipeline parallelism....

🧠 LLM Response:
 Show an overall example to deep understanding how they could be implemented in parallel deep learning.

Compared to our everyday language, parallel computing is defined as having more than one operation performed simultaneously and properly on various processors or on a single processor with multiple cores. Looking at the idea of parallel computing, multiple ways exist to achieve the end goal of having various processes executed simultaneously. There are two different parallelisms techniques in moving data between hundreds or thousands of GPUs efficiently. Two techniques are contrasted in this work studies, namely, data parallelism and model parallelism.

Data parallelism is a parallelism approach that covers performing operations such as matrix multiplications, elementarithmetic additions, and equalities comparisons on multiple identical copies of enormous datasets, with shared model weights, as shown in Fig. 1 [1]. This parallelism approach is based on the numerical computation algorithm at a layer level. Thus, each operation can be carried out independently of the model layer, allowing approximately the ideal acceleration to be achieved [1]. In Fig. 1, matrices A, W, and B are employed as model weights, data, and model outcomes [1]. A summary of data parallelism operations is found in Fig. 2 [1]. Importantly, because all weights change simultaneously during each training step across all processors in a data parallel model, forward and backward propagations occur firstly for a given input with a shared copy of matrix weights and then immediately after a given input matrix with altered shared model weights [1].

Fig. 1. The basic process mainly involved in data parallel and model parallel training methods [1]. 

Fig. 2. Data parallelism operations, where multiple large datasets are utilized. The datasets are broken down into manageable chunks used as input matrices, where each d\_i represents a different chunk of data from the larger dataset blocks, and all variables are used in each step of back and forward propagation  [1].

Model parallelism makes use of multiple model sub-graphs, with each graph defined by a different set of weights, where each subgraph has a different subset of parameters. Several weight matrices are not exactly shared among GPUs due to the varying weights matrix parameters. In Fig. 1, two types of parameters are considered during the model weights matrix split: convolutional and fully connected matrix weight parameters [1]. During partitioning, convolutional and fully connected weight matrices are segmented into sub-matrices, which is guided by the design of the neural network model [1]. Ideally, model-parallelism achieve least idle time and highest efficiency but cannot fully utilize the GPU resources, compared to data parallelism [1]. Thus, data parallelism is usually adopted in designing massively parallel deep learning [1].

A visual understanding of the idea behind data and pipeline parallelism can be observed, as shown in Fig. 1 [1]. Based on the problem posed in Fig. 1, five solutions of how data and pipeline parallelism can be applied in parallel to achieve the optimal GPU resourse utilization are presented in Fig. 3 [1]. Fig. 3a and 3b are example use cases using data and pipeline parallelisms, the shuffle of matrix `B` between both concepts, and the segment of weight or parameter matrices to best attain the overall high compute efficiency for both models [1]. Fig. 3c is an anticipated improvement in model performance by implementing the two parallelism methods into one convolutional networks model [1]. Furthermore, Fig. 3d and 3e portray how data and model parallelisms operate in large deep learning models with multiple convolutional and fully connected layers [1]. It was also noticed that five solutions from Fig. 3 do not take into consideration all neurons and connections in a full model implementation [1]. The purpose of these visual solutions is to provide a simple and easy-to-understand representation of the data and pipeline parallelism concepts.

Fig. 3. Five examples of parallelism techniques showcases utilization of data parallel and pipeline parallelism according to the defined computational structure of a deep learning neural network model, where (a) and (b) present a sample CNN model with two GPUs, with (a) combining data and model parallelisms as one CNN architecture only, and (b) applying pipeline and data parallelisms into the CNN model in parallel, but the CPU and the two GPUs are using. (c) and (d) extend their parallelism concepts to incorporate CNN models with a higher number of convolutional layers. Lastly, (e) demonstrates the data and model parallelism integration into a DNN model with multiple fully connected layers [1].

Reference：

