[24/200] Running: What is dynamic batching in inference servers?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Dynamic batching is a technique used in inference servers where incoming requests are grouped into sets that can be processed together in a single forward pass through the neural network. By doing this, the inference server can take advantage of GPU parallelism to increase throughput by a significant order of magnitude. The grouping of requests into batches is typically done on demand, and may group requests across different models and different inputs. It stops making sense to batch requests together when the computations required for each request are too dissimilar and instead a model ambiguity is introduced. Inference servers like Clipper, Cloudera, Seldon, etc. use dynamic batching to ensure high-throughput at ultra-low latencies that can scale for large data-sets during inference.

It is noted that dynamic batching is to be distinguishable during the model training phase as well as during the model inference phase:

> Dynamic batching is a concept that only really makes sense for inference. For training, because the loss needs to be minimized, it is important to have as many entries as possible since this is the signal you are trying to minimize.

To read more about dynamic batching and inference servers, refer to the following:

Papers:
Dynamic Batching for Deep Learning Inference (Infocomm 2019 version)
Dynamic batching for multi-tenant deep learning inference

GitHub:
training-serving-disparity

For specific tasks, e.g. transformer models for large data-sets, the Gemini paper provides improved methods in terms of the throughput for inference servers:
Model Parallelism as a Service for Inference from Dynamic Batching (Gemini paper)

What is Multi-Model Serving in inference servers?

Multi-Model Serving is an approach implemented in Deep Learning inference servers to handle requests that require a mix-match of different pre-trained models. An input request can refer to multiple models during inference and the inference server should be able to keep track of the sequence for the correct model execution and provide a single consolidated inference response.

> So in the life of an inference server, it can have hundreds, thousands of models deployed that all of your customers use of yours or a bunch of customers’ use. If you’re providing it as a product, it gets the model, training phase, deploys it, and now all of a sudden you have three different graphs, that form some composite model.
>
> In multi-model serving we serve less well-known, less frequently requested models and combine them with frequently served models.

Typically, these pre-trained models can be from different frameworks, e.g., TensorFlow, ONNX, PyTorch, etc. The inference server must be able to translate between the different formats, and pass the consolidated inference request to the specific execution engine for that framework. Again, depending on the execution speed of the different engines, the mixed-match inference requests are batched dynamically for processing, as described in the previous FAQ on Dynamic Batching.

Papers about multi-model serving for inference servers:
Multi-Model Serving for Accelerators
Model Parallelism as a Service for Inference from Dynamic Batching (Gemini paper)

