[26/200] Running: What is perplexity in language models and why is it importan...

🧠 LLM Response:

========

### **Perplexity**

Perplexity is a common measurement used for evaluation of language models. Basically, perplexity measures how well a probabilistic model predicts a sample. It is defined as the exponential of the cross-entropy between the true distribution and the model's distribution. Haico Beck et al. (2019) describe it as "the inverse of the geometric mean of the model's per-word probabilities for a given text".

For natural language, lower perplexity corresponds to better model performance because the model is making better statistical predictions. It's like punishing the model for being wrong, and the less it's wrong, the better it performs.

To make it more intuitive, it's easier to think about what the perplexity value represents. For example, if the perplexity is 50 for a test set, imagine that a language model is thinking of all possible next words after a given sequence and has to choose the best one. This setting is equivalent to flipping a coin 50 times and choosing a word from a finite vocabulary based on the outcome. By lowering the perplexity (for example 10), the model outputs a sequence that has higher probability of being the true next sequence.

### **Dependencies**

Perplexity only takes into account one-step dependencies, ignoring other dependencies such as n-grams or context. Models usually take past words into account (n-grams), or even previous sentences, but perplexity can not handle this dependency.


### **References**

- Haico Beck et al. (2019). [An Introduction to Language Modeling](https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.09720). arXiv preprint arXiv:1908.09720.
- Danijar Hafner et al. (2020). [Dreamer: Continuous, multimodal, hierarchical, and recurrent world models from video](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.07778). arXiv preprint arXiv:2006.07778.
- Ethan Dyer (2016). [What is perplexity and how does one compute it in terms of probability?](https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/1477858/what-is-perplexity-and-how-does-one-compute-it-in-terms-of-probability). Stack Exchange.
- Christopher Olah (2016). [Understanding LSTM Networks](https://colah.github.io/posts/2015-08-Understanding-LSTMs/). colah's blog.
- Christian Szegedy (2016). [Inceptionism: Going deeper into neural networks](https://ai.googleblog.com/2015/06/inceptionism-going-deeper-into-neural.html). Google AI blog.
[27/200] Running: How does mixed precision training benefit model training?...

🧠 LLM Response:


In today’s high-performance computing market, researchers are increasingly targeting dense Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) models, commonly powered by Graphics Processing Units (GPUs). Despite the convenience of training large models, the memory consumption on a device grows quickly with the growing number of model parameters, activation functions and gradients. In this scenario, it is important to optimize memory utilization for improved training effectiveness.

To tackle this problem, there has been a recent focus on mixed precision training, which essentially involves training models in low-precision arithmetic while simultaneously storing and updating the model parameters on GPUs at higher precision. The strategy of using lower-precision numbers for training and using mixed precision for matrix multiplication was first introduced in the seminal work of Michelangelo et al. 2017. This builds upon research from Davidson et al. 2016 and Chen et al. 2017, who used Tensor Cores to perform 4x4x4 matrix multiplication, each of which can achieve much higher floating point operation throughput (FLOPs).

Table 1. Comparing training on FP-32 and FP-16 Tensor Cores for two topology variants on ResNET-50

Training in even lower precision, however, increases the risk of overflow, underflow, and loss of activation/weight precision in both forward and backward passes, degrading the models’ performance or making them computationally infeasible. The mixed precision training strategy keeps the model training effective by maintaining most of the overall operations still running into low precision, while efficiently including certain parts of the network in higher precision. As a result, lower precision training becomes applicable to training of a wide variety of deep models, while keeping the performance stable.

Mixed precision training on NVIDIA GPU’s

NVIDIA GPUs with Tensor Core compute capabilities like V100, T4, A100 and new Quadro RTX, T1000, etc has a built-in capability to handle low precision training (fp16) for both parameters and tensors in all the recurrent connections. To allow actual implementation, NVIDIA provides APIs to help mixing precision and keeping the training's convergence.

Before mixed precision training, the models are trained on high precision weights and activations (32-bit floating point numbers). However, this process can be significantly optimized using a technique called Automatic Mixed Precision (AMP) incorporated in Tensor Cores. AMP automatically determines when to use 16-bit (fp16) and 32-bit (fp32) resources as well as handle the necessary type castings for correct computation. This is achieved without making any change to the model definition also the loss reduction layers have been handled by Tensor Compiler (for more information on AMP follow the link http://nvidia.github.io/apex/).

When the use of AMP, the memory requirement on the GPU is lowered because of the reduced size of fp16 weights – dropping Learnable Parameters (weights) from B to ~H/2 (H is the fully contiguous memory size of the weights). Because the memory consumption is lessened, a single memory expenditure (allocation) can address the effect of a larger batch size (for more information follow https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/mixed-precision-training-tensor-cores/).

For instance, FP16 activation means features that need to compute the loss function and update the model should be stored in 16-bit. Batch normalization is needed to smooth largely varying features before performing operations likeInvariant Mapping which requires FP32 precision. The. 2.2 version allows the autonomous choice of precision for activities and the training would not explode by changing any parameters. However in the test cases FP16 was preferred in all most all the cases.

