[28/200] Running: What is the function of the softmax layer in transformers?...

🧠 LLM Response:


The softmax layer in transformers is the last layer before the output layer. It takes the raw scores produced by the network and transforms them into a probability distribution over the possible classes. The probability of each class is calculated as the raw score for that class divided by the sum of all raw scores.

The softmax layer ensures that the output of the transformer is a valid probability distribution, meaning that the probabilities of all possible classes sum up to 1. This is useful for tasks such as natural language processing, where the transformer is often used to predict the next word in a sentence or the probability of a sentence being grammatical. By using the softmax layer, the transformer is able to make accurate and interpretable predictions about the input.
[29/200] Running: Explain embeddings and how they're used in NLP models....

🧠 LLM Response:


Embeddings are a type of word representation that allows words with similar meaning to have a similar representation. They are used in NLP models to capture the semantic relationships between words.

In an embedding, each word is represented as a dense vector in a continuous vector space. This vector space is learned during training and is used to capture the semantic relationships between words. For example, words that are semantically similar, such as "dog" and "puppy", will have vectors that are close to each other in the embedding space.

Embeddings are typically learned as part of the training process for a NLP model. The model is trained on a large corpus of text data, and the embedding vectors are learned as a byproduct of the training process. The embedding vectors can then be used as input to other NLP models, such as sequence-to-sequence models or transformer models.

There are several benefits to using embeddings in NLP models. First, they allow the model to capture the semantic relationships between words, which can improve the performance of the model on tasks such as text classification or machine translation. Second, embeddings are a more compact and efficient representation of words than traditional one-hot encoding schemes. This can make it easier to train NLP models on large datasets, as the models will have fewer parameters to learn.

Overall, embeddings are an important tool in NLP, as they allow models to capture the semantic relationships between words and improve the performance of NLP tasks.
