[43/200] Running: Describe how you would use RAG (Retrieval Augmented Generati...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Think if you are building a QA (Question answering) systems based on RAG. 

Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) is a technique used in natural language processing to build question answering (QA) systems. It combines the strengths of retrieval-based models and generation-based models to provide more accurate and comprehensive answers to user queries.

To use RAG for building a QA system, the following steps can be taken:

1. **Data Collection**: Collect a large corpus of text data relevant to the domain of the QA system. This data can come from various sources such as books, articles, websites, and databases.
2. **Retrieval Model Training**: Train a retrieval model on the collected data to create an index of the data. This model will be used to quickly retrieve relevant documents from the corpus in response to user queries.
3. **Generation Model Training**: Train a generation model on the collected data to generate coherent and fluent answers to user queries. The generation model can be a sequence-to-sequence model, a transformer model, or any other suitable model.
4. **RAG Model Training**: Combine the retrieval model and the generation model to create a RAG model. The RAG model learns to use the retrieved documents as context to generate more accurate and comprehensive answers to user queries. During training, the RAG model is provided with user queries, relevant documents, and gold standard answers. The model learns to generate answers that are as close as possible to the gold standard answers.
5. **Inference**: During inference, the RAG model receives user queries and uses the retrieval model to retrieve relevant documents from the corpus. The RAG model then uses the retrieved documents as context to generate an answer to the user query using the generation model.
6. **Evaluation**: Evaluate the performance of the RAG model using various metrics such as BLEU, ROUGE, METEOR, and Precision@k. These metrics measure the similarity between the generated answers and the gold standard answers.

RAG has several advantages over traditional QA systems. It can provide more comprehensive answers to user queries by using relevant documents as context. It can also handle ambiguous queries by retrieving multiple relevant documents and generating answers that take into account the information in all the documents. Additionally, RAG can handle out-of-distribution queries by retrieving and generating answers based on relevant documents, even if the documents are not in the training data.
[44/200] Running: What is streaming inference and how is it used?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Streaming inference is where models perform predictions on partial sequences of a pipeline over time. This is used primarily in three areas: audio, video and text. The most common use cases for streaming inference in video include object detection and image classification. In video, frames are being captured at a high frame rate, so it would be computationally very expensive to buffer long sequences of video and process them all. Instead, what the model typically does is it performs predictions on little buffers, which can be anything from one-tenth of a second to a second of video, and then will output predictions on a partial sequence using the context of the already buffered video. Then, as new video comes in, the model overwrites the older portions of the buffer and shifts the buffers so that it always has a little bit of history that it stays aware of, but it's not keeping track of all the previous frames that were observed.

In the case of audio, streaming inference is commonly used in speech transcription pipelines, known as speech-to-text within computer vision, where models try to identify the sound or sounds being made from the waveform of the audio. Just as with video, with audio, it's necessary to capture large chunks of audio and then predict from those chunks of audio over time. With text data, streaming inference is commonly used in applications such as speech recognition, where the spoken phrase can be incorporated into a buffer such that many phrases for a conversation or a meeting can be incorporated into that buffer, so that the model can track the context of the conversation that has been happening.

One other example that I think is particularly fascinating is given in the world of health care. Imagine you have a diagnosis application where medical transcriptions from clinicians are coming in. Oftentimes, there are certain words in medical transcriptions that are very important to identify, such as specific names of drugs or names of different illnesses. Using buffering, it is possible for the models to identify those words over time, even if they came out of order, for example, because the doctor mentioned them in passing earlier in the conversation with the patient, but only mentioned their importance later in the conversation. This type of context, or contextual modeling, is one of the use cases that streaming inference enables.

How does streaming inference differ from "regular" inference?

In "regular" inference, you can generally assume that you will have the full context of whatever input was given to the model. That's what generally goes on in the "training time" of the model. During training time, designers of models for the purposes of computer vision, natural language processing, or audio will train their models on a very large dataset consisting of a series of short-term examples, and that's kind of the design paradigm learned during regular training time.

The design paradigm is very different for streaming in that, during inference time, the model has to be capable of taking partial sequences of a pipeline and making predictions using the context of whatever just came in. That differs dramatically from the paradigms used in training time. So, for designers of models, the primary difference is modeling design of the architecture.

For users of models, using streaming inference is very similar to using regular inference. The user essentially just calls the "predict" function and passes along an audio, video or text chunk, just as they would in regular inference, although in the case of streaming inference, that could possibly be shifted over frames.

If updated data could change the classification, why only operate on partial sequences?

It is fundamentally a trade-off between latency and accuracy. Performing predictions on partial sequences tends to have less latency than buffering the full pipeline or full sequence. But on the other hand, it may not have as high accuracy as if the full sequence were available to it. There are also presumably computational expenses for incorporating all of that data and raising the computational requirements of the inference pipeline.

It's possible to buff the full sequences of a pipeline. The streaming model itself doesn't prevent it, it's just that, practically speaking, the latency would increase dramatically because more information would be required in order to make a prediction, as there would be more context needed. The model would have to be able to incorporate all of that data. The model itself does not make the decision in this trade-off. The model architect chooses the volume of the buffer because it represents the best design trade-off in terms of the accuracy that the model will be able to get, given the context that can be incorporated into the model, and the latency that the model can achieve based on its user requirements.

