🧠 LLM Response:
 They are, in fact, attention mechanisms of hybrid posterior and prior computations where vision meets language. Prior computations involve stacking together information from tokens from a sequence in context and posterior computations involve applying the stacked information to a single token or forming relative positions of other tokens.

For this to understand, consider the problem being solved here: how can we predict the next token \(y_{t+1}\). Mechanisms such as LSTMs-simple and LSTMs-peephole cannot explain much about how to predict the next token, as they continue to grab information from different directions. Of course, LSTMs-peephole also grab information from the input but compare that against the complexity of the token representation available today. The inputs are no longer integers referencing tokens from a sequences, but rather real valued vectors that capture language-specific context for predicting next tokens.

A unified mechanism, called flash attention, solves both these computations at the same time while having some additional nifty features for self-attention.Consider the general setting for language prediction tasks:

For the given input:\(X={x_1,x_2,{\dots},x_m} \in 	exttt{R}^{m x d\_model}\)
where each \(x_i\) is a \(d\_{model}\) length vector describing the information available for a specific token. This information is represented as a vector made of the following key, once again represented as vectors of length \(d\_{model}\):

- \(k_i={K\_1, \dots,K\_i,{\dots},K\_m} \btinR^{m \times d\_model}\), which is the key for token i.
- \(v\_i={V\_1, \dots,V\_i,{\dots},V\_m} \btinR^{m \times d\_model}\), which is the value for token i.
- \(p\_i={P\_1, \dots,P\_i,{\dots},P\_m} \btinR^{m \times d\_model}\), which is the position for token i.

Our query q sequences are represented by \(Q={q\_1,q\_2, \dots, q\_t, \dots q\_n} \btinR^{n \times d\_model}\). Here, the subscript t denotes the index for token for which we are currently looking for a possible solution.

For now, let us focus only on the the initial input X. This input, before being sent into the attention layers, needs to be normalized. This is necessary for\[Attention(X)=\sum_{i=1}^{m}\alpha ik\_i\]where \(\alpha\) is the attention score for token ki. This attention score is determined by taking the dot product\[\alpha\_i = K\_i \cdot Q = k\_i^ \t T \cdot Q\] The dot product between ki and Q (or any two vectors) can be thought of as a matrix multiplication between two matrices \(K\_i \in 	exttt{R}^{1 \times d\_model}\) and Q \(\in 	exttt{R}^{n \times d\_model}\) respectively. This particular size of the dot product means the output is \(n\) in length. This is specific to Flash attention and adds a bit of flexibility: only one operation is being performed, which destroys tokens on both ends and prioritises a self attention score (or can be thought of as a vanilla measure of similarity) for an entire query sequence against another token for attention.

Another interesting aspect of this approach is the ability to perform multi-head attention simply by concatenating several of the above dot product systems. Supposing that we would want to keep k attention locations fixed for all of the n time steps, that is equivalent to applying the dot product n times. This number of dot products may vary for a given problem.

