[61/200] Running: What is a causal attention mask?...

🧠 LLM Response:

---------------------------------

When you want to analyse a particular neuron (or group of neurons), you might
filter out all context and focus on the small subset that you're interested
in (see `this blog post
<https://lila-hiifilm.github.io/2020-11-11/Causal-Masking/>`__ for a
discussion of why that is important).

A causal attention mask,
^
shown below with the caveman attention mechanism, does exactly that by zero-ing
out all neutrons that do not "cause" the focus neurons. In this example,
the caveman only looks at pixels R1, R2, and R3, and pays no attention to the
remainder (only the square of 3 pixels is drawn).

Assuming a subset contains a neuron in the focus, you can always find a
*causal* subset (or causal subset of excitations) for each neuron by
iterating from the bottom up (i.e. *causally*, i.e. the data has to become
available before you look at) till you have seen all excitations you
need.

Because there is no backwards info flow in this model, its OK to filter
out context on a neuron-by-neuron basis, although for ease-of-visualization
in 2d convolutional models, it is advantageous to filter at the level of
the blocks (so that each block can be processed-, mine- and processed
as a single square in batch processing). It is only after all excitations
(of a particular interesting neuron) have been processed that context can
be dropped.

Also, it is only OK to analyze only a small proportion of the network in this
manner, because that makes sure that removing certain neurons and making
sure the context is correct does not alter the interpretation of a specific
neuron by altering its neighbours.

Causal attention masks are currently implemented in the :code:` HiIFilm` class
(in the top layer) under the :code:` HiIImage` concentration module. The
concentration module currently only supports the :code:` HiIScale` class,
which implements the proportionate attention mechanism (that chooses the
square with the largest num_neurons).

.. code:: ipython3

    %matplotlib inline
    from Analyser.ConcentrateHiI.Caveman import HiIAttCaveman
    from Analyser.LessICS import HiIScale

    example_image = np.zeros((48,48), np.float32)
    example_image[4,3]=0.01

    caveman = HiIAttCaveman(100, example_image)
    print(caveman.__str__())


