GLSL POWERS Multi-Render Passes

A relatively new approach to depth map creation, used only by a few innovative rendering engines (like Redshift and Octane), is a beautiful construct known as fused rendering kernels.

The jittery, 1950s-computer style frame rate in the demo below finally feels like it belongs to the past. This is how you can create a full depth map without losing a single drop of speed in the game viewport. No corners have been cut; this demo takes place in real-time with all features, physics, and render elements rendering in real-time. No dynamic load/unload has been used here, so you have no restrictions while you are using it. One glorious rendering kernel to rule them all!

InGLSL, we have the option to create KERNELS on the fly and render custom multi-passes. This is extremely important for realistic rendering pipelines. First, render the barely visible fur; fine-tune the hairs by taking a second pass; and, finally, render the fur with even more detail in a third pass.

Nearly everything runs in parallel with the GPU. With this approach, there's little of the inefficiency you'd find in the old-school methods. The best part? You have a clean approach to glorious real-time rendering performance, and a full set of tools to calculate depth, area shadows, motion blur, and much more, as separate rendering tasks.

