[88/200] Running: Describe token streaming latency optimization....

🧠 LLM Response:


Token streaming latency optimization refers to the process of reducing the time delay between the generation of a message and its delivery to the recipient in a real-time messaging system that uses a token-based approach.

Token-based systems involve the use of tokens that are passed between nodes to regulate access to shared resources, such as a message queue. In such systems, the time taken to pass a token between nodes can introduce latency, especially in high-throughput scenarios where many messages are generated in a short period.

To optimize token streaming latency, various techniques can be employed, such as:

1. Reducing the number of nodes involved in the token passing process: The more nodes involved in the token passing process, the greater the overall latency. By reducing the number of nodes, the latency can be minimized.
2. Increasing the token passing frequency: The frequency at which tokens are passed between nodes can be increased to reduce the time delay between message generation and delivery.
3. Using dedicated hardware: Specialized hardware, such as field-programmable gate arrays (FPGAs) or application-specific integrated circuits (ASICs), can be used to accelerate the token passing process and reduce latency.
4. Employing caching techniques: Caching frequently accessed data can help reduce the time required to retrieve it, thus reducing overall latency.
5. Implementing load balancing: Load balancing techniques can be used to distribute traffic evenly across multiple nodes, reducing the load on any single node and minimizing latency.
6. Adopting a more efficient token passing protocol: The token passing protocol itself can be optimized to reduce latency. For example, a protocol that minimizes the number of messages required to pass a token between nodes can help reduce latency.
7. Applying compression techniques: Compressing data before it is transmitted can help reduce the amount of data that needs to be passed between nodes, thus reducing the time required for token passing.

By employing these and other optimization techniques, token streaming latency can be minimized, enabling real-time messaging systems to operate with greater efficiency and reliability.
[89/200] Running: How to evaluate hallucinations in language models?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 • r/MachineLearning
Hallucinations, generated outputs which are downright false, is a known issue in language models, but how to evaluate them in a systematic way?
Hallucination is different to random noise, it suggests that some model's internal process went haywire and systematically produced an output which is incorrect. Some examples could be "8 + 8 is 37." or "The capital of France is London.", while random noise may produce outputs which are a complete mess.
In the image domain, hallucination could be referred to as "seeing shapes that aren't there" or more technically, chimeric images. To measure hallucination in the image domain, I would have used metrics such as false positive pixel rate, where the false negative is ignored, as I want to know if the model can "see" things that aren't there.
However, in the text domain, it is not as clearcut.
GPT-3 and other language models are big and noisy enough, that if you measure word error rate between generated and true, if you're designing the system to always generate an output including all the words that are in the prompt, even if it is a simple, "The capital is London.", it will always have high errors compared with other simpler models. You can ignore the word error rate on the prompt, but it could lead to false positive where good models which generate prompts get penalized.
What then can be a good metric to measure hallucination during text generated between 2 models? The scores should ideally be comparable across a wide range of input sequences and model outputs.
If possible, I would appreciate it if someone could point to existing work or ideas on this.
submitted by shuubhi for the subreddit r/MachineLearning
an hour ago by ml_explorers 1
This problem falls under the large umbrella of Out of Distribution (OOD) detection in ML. But specifically for pretrained language models, there have been some works on it (since NLP has a lot of domain-specific OOD).
H start https://huggingface.co/spaces/wandb/grouped-ood can be a good starting point. W&B has summarized a few papers and thoughts here: https://wandb.ai/wandb/grouped-ood/reports/Out-of-Distribution-Detection-for-Natural-Language-Models--Vmlldzo0NjM1MTc5
Otherwise you can checkout my EMNLP paper: https://aclanthology.org/2021.emnlp-main.558.pdf. Here we use density models and other tricks such as ensembles to classify OOD.
If you are ok with certain inductive biases during training, you can checkout: https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.431/
Methodology used to determine hallucination:
Most methods that I have seen measure similarity of the language model output to a more humans like output or rerefernce output. There is a good battery of reference-less metric in NLP, but there are no reference-less metric to determine if the language model is hallucinating in rrerefernce to a human writing.
For example, BLEURT, BERTScore and ROUGE-L style metrics could determine a language model that is producing real garbage and is having a hallucination by measuring how far (or close) it is to a reference sequence.
But these metrics might be poor at determining if the language model is hallucinating interesting things or wrong things.
If a Language Model is Hallucinating:
1. Check if parts of your prompt and/or conditioner are in the generated text, and that it has not been changed.
2. If your sequence is different than your prompt text but it is logical check if it is changing the generation in a human like way, in a way that is consistent through the sequence.
3. Finally, check if the language model output is disfluent in syntactic and semantic ways. Check out syntactic and semantic intrusion test to measure disfluance.
RShttps://nlp.stanford.edu/computational-psycholinguistics/syntactic-intrusion.html
While metrics like BERTScore, bleurt and rouge-l can give an impression of how similar a generation is to a prompt/reference, they can not determine if a model is hallucinating.
Note: hallucination can be conceptualized by analyzing if
