If `bos_token_id` has meaning to represent the separation point, why isn’t `eos_token_id` also used consistently between `train_tokenizer` and `conversation_response_tokenizer`?

===

`bos_token_id` is used as a [sentence start token](https://huggingface.co/transformers/master/model_doc/bert.html#tokenclsidfullform) for a given input sequence. It is used to denote the boundary of a sentence or span of input text for NLP tasks like token classification and extractive QA.

It does not have a direct relationship with the `eos_token_id` and their usage may vary depending on the specific tokenizer implementation. For example, the `train_tokenizer` may not use `eos_token_id` for single segment inputs, but it may be used in cases where multiple segments are concatenated using a special separator token (e.g. `[SEP]` in BERT) to mark the end of the first segment and the beginning of the second segment.

On the other hand, the `conversation_response_tokenizer` may not use the `eos_token_id` at all, since the response tokens are typically generated one at a time by the model, and there is no need to mark the end of a sequence in this context.

In summary, `bos_token_id` is used to denote the start of a sentence or input span, while `eos_token_id` may be used to mark the end of a sequence or segment, but their usage can vary depending on the specific tokenizer and task at hand.
