[93/200] Running: How does instruction following improve model usability?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 User-friendly programs such as smart speakers and web applications provide guidance on how to interact with them. For example, user manuals and voice commands show people how to operate and interact with the programs. By providing instruction guidance, people are able to interface with these programs in safe and intuitive ways, because they understand how the programs (should) work.
Conversely, if a program does not provide instruction guidance or if the guidance is unclear, then people may be unable to interact with it. This lack of instruction guidance is especially problematic for complex systems like AI models (e.g., predictive models), where people may assume that the model only works in a specific, narrow way.
To make AI models more usable in practice, we argue that instruction-following should be explicitly incorporated into AI models for the sake of (1) education and access; (2) improving trust; and (3) reducing fears and anxiety.

1. Education and access. Intuitive instruction-following methods enable a broad user base without the need for technical expertise to use AI models effectively. For example, an instruction-following AI model can provide expected inputs, possible outputs, and interpretation of results for users. Doing so allows people to access and use AI models in their own personal projects or in their work, regardless of the user’s level of technical proficiency.
2. Improving trust. Instruction-following abilities of AI models improve users’ trust and willingness to further engage with these models. For example, a study using a chatbot found that many users initially resisted using the chatbot because of the lack of instruction guidance on how to operate it.  By making users feel more at ease and in control of AI models, instruction-following abilities can enhance the overall user satisfaction and trust in how AI models can help them.
3. Reducing fears and anxiety. Instruction-following abilities of AI models can reduce user fears and anxiety that otherwise might result from interacting with a “powerful” and technically complex algorithm. By demonstrating a social knowledge of its own capabilities that can be described to users, a model that incorporates explicit instruction-following abilities is less likely to be viewed as an inscrutable, intimidating black box.

Broadly, instruction-following abilities for AI models are necessary for increasing their accessibility and usability for a wider swath of individuals who may benefit from their predictive capabilities. By addressing user concerns in practical, socially understandable ways, instruction following abilities might even increase the general public’s trust in AI models and prevent unnecessary fears and anxieties.

How might we use instruction-following work to make models more ethical and fair? Instruction-following is a person’s ability to follow direction prompts that explicitly tell a person how to do a task or expert behavior.  For example, instruction-following is explicitly telling someone to “left turn at the corner,” or “pour water from the right hand side of the beaker into the left side.”  These direction prompts, also known as fiat instructions, are a primary means of human communication.

In fact, robots and agents, like humans, can also follow high-level instruction/direction prompts, which provide them with a basic level of “understanding” on how to perform a given task or behavior.  In theory, humans using a model can suggest model behavior via instruction prompts. As a result, instruction prompts can be a useful communication tool between humans and models.

Human operators can suggest that a model acts a fair and ethical manner by providing the model with fiat instructions on what the expected fair and ethical behavior would be. For example, an instruction prompt can be used to tell a facial recognition model to minimize differences between aging and ethnicity.

The communication style of instruction prompts (“do this and then do that” in a loop) also inherently involves focusing on sequential problems. As a result, human operators can also gatekeep aspects of model behavior by order of sequential instructions.  This allows for users to better control when a possible potentially unethical or unfair behavior of the model—which might include its learning from unfit data samples—occurs.

Advantages of instruction-based AI models. Instruction-based AI models offer a number of advantages:

- A more user-friendly learning experience. Model training can be made more user-friendly by using instruction prompts. For example, a novice machine learning developer might not know how to train a model so that it fair and performs well. Instruction-based AI models help scaffold this development process by suggested desirable actions in the form of fiat instructions.
- A more understandable model. Instruction-based AI models
[94/200] Running: What is a sequence-to-sequence model?...

🧠 LLM Response:

A sequence-to-sequence model is a neural network architecture that converts a sequence of input tokens to a sequence of output tokens, such as converting the words of a sentence into the words of another sentence.
It consists of two main components: an encoder that takes the input sequence and generates a fixed-length vector representation, and a decoder that takes the fixed-length vector and generates the output sequence.
It is also called encoder-decoder model.

![sequence-to-sequence model architecture](https://miro.medium.com/max/1400/1*brN5koU6X4Lw7vQbJw4cJg.png)

One of the popular application of sequence-to-sequence model is Neural Machine Translation, where an input sequence is a sentence in one language, and the output sequence is the translation of that sentence in another language.

In summary, the sequence-to-sequence model is a powerful tool for variety of sequence-to-sequence tasks such as machine translation, text summarization, dialogue generation, and speech recognition. However, the main drawback of sequence-to-sequence model is that the fixed-length vector representation of the input sequence can't capture the whole information of the sequence especially for long sequences. In order to overcome that, Architectures like Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN) or Transformer can be used as an alternative.
