	+ FPGA DOES NOT COMPUTE MEANINGFUL signal measurement

- (Off-Target) Profiling FPGA Hardware: Clocks, FPGA Hub, Switch-to-Switch, Egress-for-Packet (Logical event trace), OpenCL API
	+ Pros: less extra-hardware required for FPGA
	+ Cons: potential emulation layer over-head

- (On-Target) Profiling ASIC Hardware: Clocks, Temperature, Power, Logic Utilization, HW Utilization, Latency, Instantaneous throughput, accumulated throughput.
   - Like FPGA on-target, but don't have the abstraction layer IPT

- (Off-Target) Profiling ASIC Hardware: Clocks, Logical event trace

### Virtualization

- (Off-Target) Power consumption measurement
	+ Pros: easy to measure
	+ Cons: not accurate ?

- Switch <-> Network Profiling / Switch-to-Switch / NIC <-> Fabric

- (On-Host) RTL Profiling
	+ Pros: easy to probe
	+ Cons: Clock and temperature may not be accurate
	+     - An over-head over emulation / synthesizer

- (On-Host) Baseline

- (Off-Host) Baseline

#### FPGA Virtualization

- (On-Host) RTL profiling
- (On-Host) Reference SW model
	+ Pros: Least overhead of any method
	+ Cons: Does not reflect exec on-target (runtime compilation)
	+ also may not be multi-threaded

#### ASIC Virtualization: Caching

- (On-Host) ARM as a Hardware / Virtualizer Harness
	+ Pros: Easy to access emulator
	+ Cons: Less accurate

- (On-Host) Emulator

### RTL level

- 1. RTL level profiling using host.
- 2. RTL level profiling using simulators
	+ Pros: No over-head because design is being executed in simulation
	+ Cons: Synthesizer may run poorly
- 3. RTL level profiling using FPGA emulation
	+ Pros: Allow accurate measurement in much less time
	+ Cons: Runs on the FPGA the exact same profile could change when the design is finally on-target

