[142/200] Running: Explain the relationship between prompt length and latency....

🧠 LLM Response:


Prompt length is the amount of time between when you give a command and the time when the system responds to that command. Latency is the time between when you give a command and the time when the system starts working on that command. Longer prompts generally result in longer latencies because the system needs more time to process and respond to longer commands. However, once the system starts working on a command, the actual processing time may be the same regardless of the length of the prompt. This is because the system has already parsed the command and determined what needs to be done, so the length of the prompt no longer affects the processing time. Overall, prompt length can have a significant impact on latency, but it does not necessarily affect the actual processing time once the system has started working on a command.
[143/200] Running: What is batch inference and why is it faster?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Batch inference refers to a scenario in which multiple input samples—or a batch of samples—are processed in one shot, as opposed to each input being processed one after the other, also known as “online inference.” The reason batch inference is faster is because it is more efficient to compute many forward passes through a neural network at the same time rather than doing it one-by-one. This is especially true on GPUs, where compute resources are more efficiently used when doing multiple computations at the same time (batching).

Batch normalization, a technique often applied in convolutional neural networks (CNNs), for instance, is better behaved when used with a batch size larger than one. This means that in some cases, it can be hard or impossible for certain architectures to be used with an online inference setup.

Even in the case of techniques for which the use of batches is optional, such as recurrent neural networks (RNNs), batching often results in a significant speedup compared to online inference.

Assuming you have many samples to process, you can take this further and group samples that are similar in terms of how they are used in the forward pass of the neural network and process them together (e.g., based on having the same length or sharing attention patterns). This technique is called micro-batching—call it “meta batching” if you like—and it is used in some of the more advanced ASR engines to further speed-up inference.

Batch inference is used not only during put-training but also during post-processing and decoding (including similarity search). When trying to locate the phoneme near pixel (i), in (a) you see that you have to move through a batch of pixels (i-1), … (i-w/2), whereas in (b) you can see all the pixels that contain that phoneme at the same time. So in each case, we use the most convenient strategy for the context of that particular operation.

To take advantage of batching, consider that sometimes there might be multiple processes involved (e.g., the ASR system is expected to return the top-10 hypotheses, or a teacher model is expected to rank students’ answers based on quality). In that case, it pays to be aware of the specific pipelining limitations (the most important being how many operations can be performed in parallel, for example, limited by GPU memory), and group together the computations that cause the least possible idle time.

Pipelining the computations at test time is very different from pipelining operations at training time. At test time inference, pipelining is performed to efficiently use the resources available to you through parallelization, whereas at training time the goal is not only to parallelize computation but also to parallelize a lot of other things that cannot be trivially solved. Optimizations are typically based on the ability to store the data on disk (or in some other type of memory), reduce I/O overheads, and so on. In practical terms, during training you require more batch norm / DP normalization computations, etc., which are not necessary in the context of inference. As such, inference is always far more computationally efficient than training.

Fully-connected (FC) layers

Fully-connected (FC) layers, also commonly called density layers or affine layers, exist in most popular deep learning architectures. They are a type of layer that just multiply each memory by learned matrix. The terms FC layers, densely-connected layers, and affine layers are mostly used interchangeably.

In the context of FC layers, there’s a minor terminological discrepancy between traditional deep learning terminology and that used in Seq2Seq models [Bahdanau et al., 2014]. In the context of deep learning, FC layers are fully invertible, meaning that you have the same dimensions of input as the output. In the context of Seq2Seq models, FC layers are not invertible, however, because the mapping is from a fixed-size vector to a decoder-state sequence. For example the Seq2Seq model [Chorowski et al., 2015] uses a dense encoding in which a fixed-size vector X is associated to the encoded sequence: X > > > [x1, x2, x3] > > > x1…3. For these citizens of the Seq2Seq model world, if you reverse the layers in the model you end up with an FC layer between each decoder state.

For simplicity I talk about FC layers as invertible layers (connected with units rather
[144/200] Running: What are rotary sin-cos embeddings?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 They represent the rotation using a 2-DOF holonomic drive in the Athena II arm (a 5-jointed arm based on the Jacobian). The rotation is represented as an element in the $SO(3)$ group, using two components, a parameter of the Plücker coordinates that is a cosine of the axial angle, and a second coordinate a sinusoid of the natural angle (the rotation angle). Both coordinates are learned via two sinusoidal regression network outputs, controlled by a single hidden layer feedforward neural network. This is the Athena II arm:

They are also used to simplify the control representation from 6-DOF full rotation control (position and orientation) to 4-DOF yaw/pitch/roll angular control and end effector control. This is useful for robotic grasping, using the following diagram to represent what angular control is required for the Athena II arm.

For a specific system example (in Athena II), the rotation is represented as a multiplication of two parameters, $(s,c)$ from two sinusoidal networks with amplitude and phase 0 as follows: $s = \sin (\hat{s})$ and $c = \cos(\hat{c})$. The rotation matrix would then be:

$R = \left| {\begin{array}{*{20}{c}} c^2 \left( {1 – s} \right) + s&{s c \left( {1 – c} \right)}&{c s}\\ {s c \left( {1 – c} \right)}&{s^2 \left( {1 – c} \right) + c}&{c s}\\ { – cs}&{ – cs}&{c^2 + s^2} \end{array}} \right|$

where c and s are the common shorthands for $\cos(\theta)$ and $\sin(\theta)$, with rotation here in the z-axis with $\theta = \hat{c}(1 – c) + \hat{s} s$.

Let’s write code in TF 2.0 to represent this:

 What does this code do ? It is a wrapper over a class and input_fn functions that conditionally creates a Grasp Network or a PointGoal Network that are demonstrated in the previous half.

It involves reading the Tensorflow version and then creating an Athena network that conditionally calls the Grasp Network or the PointGoal Network depending on what network type is called. The input_fn function also conditionally calls the input_fn that matches the network specified.

The Grasp Network uses the Rotary Sin-Cos embedding parameters of the arm and then concatenates that with other parameters to define a Grasp Network. Most of the functions used here are covered in the previous half.

There are primarily two parts to this:

- Embed actions for positive and negative actions required from the joints (determined by the following code from joint_controller.py):

- Embed actions for actions available during grasping mode like opening the gripper or having a single arm movement (done through code from Panda.py):

And that’s it! That’s how the whole thing is implemented. Following below are some plots of how the policies learned perform:

