[158/200] Running: What is a quantized model and how is it served?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 How does it differ from other models?

The quantized model is an inference model that is served through AWS SageMaker Edge Manager, which provides tools to secure, monitor, and manage deployments of machine learning (ML) models on edge devices. This model is typically used in deep learning applications and runs inference on images, videos, audio, or (tabular) data. These models are quantized for efficient execution on resource-limited edge devices and to reduce bandwidth usage for model updates from edge devices to the cloud.

Quantization is a post-training optimization that transforms the weights and activations of an inference model to use fewer bits, which reduces the model size and lowers the computational complexity, which helps to conserve energy and memory. The reduction in the model size allows a faster deployment of the model over the air (OTA) to edge devices and enables the storage and processing of multiple small models instead of one larger model.

Compared to floating-point and integer-based models, quantized models are smaller in size and faster for inference. They can run efficiently on CPUs without the need for dedicated hardware, and on some devices they can even run faster on ARM CPUs than on GPU hardware.

SageMaker Edge Manager packages these quantized models along with an efficient deep learning library such as TensorFlow Lite, PyTorch Mobile, or TVM and deploys them to the AWS IoT Device Defender for device management and updates. You can also serve regular floating-point and integer-based models on AWS SageMaker Edge Manager as well as quantized models.
