[160/200] Running: What is warmup time in inference pipelines?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Warmup time is the time taken to prepare and cache internal execution plans and, optionally, fine-tune model weight values in the inference engine so that subsequent requests can be executed with minimal latency. This time is typically amortized over a high number of inference requests, so it is usually not perceived by end users. However, for applications that rely on low latency for each individual inference request such as some questions Answering systems, automotive safety use cases, or cloud-based services that expect low latency inference from the get-go, understanding and managing the warmup time of your deployed pipelines is indispensable.

Why is there a warmup time in inference pipelines?

In an inference pipeline, during the first inference request, a wide range of preparatory tasks are involved. These include essentially configuring the pipeline, loading the model to memory, parsing and optimizing the model, preprocessing input data, validating and pre- caching a plan for efficiently using the underlying hardware resources, and fine-tuning the model's weight values (also known as model fine-tuning). Preprocessing input data and fine-tuning the model weights can be optional. The amount of time spent on each of the tasks depends on several hardware and software factors, but in general, preparing and caching the execution plan takes the most time.

In the following section, we will briefly walk through each of the tasks and explore potential factors that can influence warmup time and make it excessively long. We will also discuss various ways you can manage and optimize the warmup time of your pipelines.

I. Model loading time

One of the first major contributors to warmup time is loading the model into memory. The time it takes to perform this task depends on a few factors such as the model size itself and the underlying hardware resources, specifically the storage and memory hierarchy.

As models grow larger in size (millions and billions of parameters), loading models into memory from disk s could take a significant amount of time, comparable to the total inference time for smaller models. Loading a large model into memory from a traditional spinning disk, for example, could take anywhere from tens to hundreds of seconds, compared to a few seconds on devices with solid-state storage (such as an SSD). Loading the model from a local or network-attached storage device can also significantly increase latency, due to additional I/O related to accessing the data across a network or physical disk.

Factors to minimize model loading time:

- Use the largest local storage that is available to you, such as a high-capacity SSD.
- Amortize the time cost of loading over multiple requests, using a queueing or prefetching architecture.
- Preload the model information (such as the model layout, inputs, and outputs) if the pipeline configuration is known at compile time, instead of loading it from an external file before each request.

II. Parsing and optimizing model time

Before executing an inference pipeline, its internal structure needs to be understood and optimized to produce an integrated task graph (also known as the computational graph) that represents the mathematical operations performed by the pipeline. The goal here is to transform the input model, written in a higher-level format (such as ONNX) that may require additional parsing or analysis, into a lower level format (such as a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) of hardware accelerator-specified kernels) that can be directly dispatched to hardware.

This step, which usually takes a single thread, can be I/O bounded (due to model file access) or CPU bounded (due to model complexity), depending on factors such as model format, model file storage location, and model complexity. The time taken by this step can vary from a few to hundreds of milliseconds, depending on these factors.

To reduce the parsing and optimization time, consider the following best practices and techniques:

- Use compact, machine-optimized model formats such as TensorFlow-Lite or TensorRT as much as possible.
- Use a local storage device with high IOPS (such as an SSD) to read the model file in parallel threads (if possible).
- If possible, use hardware-specific tools (such as TensorRT or TensorFlow XLA compiler) to fuse and optimize kernels. This can significantly reduce the number of graph nodes and potentially lower the latency.
- Consider using platform-specific or third-party tools to pre-parse the model file during pipeline compilation, so that the pre-parsed format can be easily consumed by the execution engine during warmup.
