[164/200] Running: What are server cold starts in model serving?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Cloud providers like AWS place host servers in an idle pool when they are not running any user request. Due to their idle state, they are a bit sluggish when a cold start request is made. The cold start can lead to longer latencies, which affects the overall service performance metrics. Depending on the cloud provider, this sluggishness could take anywhere from 100ms to 4 seconds. As a model server developer, you want to solve these factors to improve your service latencies.

In this article, we will explain the impact of server cold starts in model serving and detail some practices to mitigate it.

How can you tell your server is being cold started?

You can find this out by printing a timestamp when your service receives a request to see the latency difference between the service handling a regular request and a cold-started one. Typically, a cold start can take over 100ms, but certain environments can cause it to last longer. If you suspect your server is taking longer due to a cold start, print the timestamp in the various stages where you think the cold start is occurring.

5 Best practices for servers to mitigate cold-start latency in model serving

## 1. Keep your containerizer always ready.

There are two ways to address this. One way is to configure your cloud provider to keep a few idle instances ready with your service. That ensures when a new request comes in, it can use an already warm instance. However, that implies a more significant cost for running these idle instances.

Another way, that lower costs, is to have a containerizer that quickly starts your service whenever a cold start is triggered. Containers are better than VMs for this purpose since VMs need more time to prepare the machine for the application. But containers do not come without any downsides. Containers are usually small, and so data spilling can happen often. Data spilling is when your data does not fit within the cached container, forcing it to fetch from the storage and hence increasing the latencies. An optimum solution would balance cost with the cold start latencies and ensure containers can scale up and down without much overhead.

How Seldon provides cold start mitigation

Seldon uses Kubernetes, which is a widely used container orchestrator. Kubernetes keeps pods in the “ready phase” for faster start up times so that cold starts take minimal time to start. In addition, it helps utilize cloud spot instances (when available) to further reduce the cold start latencies.

## 2. Reduce the container size by reducing the model size.

An intuitive solution to cold start latency is to reduce your service size, and you can achieve this by reducing your model size. Model pruning, quantization, distillation, and compression techniques are a few standard methods available in the model compression space. Model compression algorithms help reduce the model size and make your service more efficient, and thus the server can handle requests faster.

Also, make sure you use model binary formats like ONNX instead of source code like PYTORCH. Binary formats are more efficient than source code because the deserialization is done in C/C++ which is much faster than python deserialization.

Pro tip: Reduce the model size to improve the overall service performance by a significant margin without losing much model accuracy.

## 3. Warmer-your instance when idle.

Another way to mitigate cold starts is to periodically invoke a request to keep the instances warm. This practice may not help with prediction throughput since you will get more cold starts when your idle instances are not in use, but it will make sure that the instances that are being used have smaller latencies.

Depending on your use case scenario, you can choose to either periodically wake up instances using a scheduler or invoke wake-up requests by using some light-weighted preprocessing tasks. Keep in mind that the scheduler-based technique requires coordination and has more overhead, whereas the pre-processing based technique can run inefficiencies but is not very coordinated.

How Seldon warms-up servers for you.

Seldon Core combats server cold starts by periodically invoking wake-up requests to warm up the instances. Seldon uses a lazy approach and only warms up the instances when they are ready for a new request. Therefore, it efficiently uses your system resources, making warm-ups faster and less wasteful.

## 4. Initialize your components during idle times.

When get_model() method initializes, it increases the initialization time. When you know your get_model() API will take time
[165/200] Running: Explain how to avoid padding inefficiencies....

🧠 LLM Response:


Padding inefficiency can occur when algorithmic operations are applied to data stored in the cloud. Because these data blocks must be arranged in fixed-sized blocks, sometimes these operations have to perform calculations on blocks of data that are partially filled with padding zeroes because of the fixed block sizes.

To avoid these problems, there are two possible strategies:

1. Use variable-sized data chunks or blocks as opposed to the fixed block sizes employed by most cloud storage systems. By using variable-sized blocks, you eliminate the need for padding data and ensure that computations are only performed on the data you're interested in.
2. Perform computations on the data before you upload it to the cloud. This way, you ensure that the data you upload is only the data that you need. This reduces the amount of wasted storage space and eliminates the need for padding inefficiencies.

Of course, both of these strategies have their drawbacks. Using variable-sized blocks can result in increased complexity and overhead, while precomputing data on your local system can be time-consuming and resource-intensive. However, by carefully balancing these tradeoffs, you can minimize the impact of padding inefficiencies on your cloud-based computing workloads.
[166/200] Running: How does tokenizer pre-processing affect TTFT?...

🧠 LLM Response:

We find that some tokenizers cause TTFT events. The existence of TTFT appears to be because the tokenizer defines certain words as invalid terms, and replace these terms with `<unk>` tokens before training. These `<unk>` tokens end up in the vocab and are used in training: usually this is fine, but in certain cases it can lead to shorter file checkpoints being produced, as all terms replaced by `<unk>` have titles of one letter.

This is very problematic in two ways:
- Generating a title of one letter can sometimes cause a TTFT, depending on the checkpoint handler and their behavior when met with a file that has short file names.
- Even if no TTFT is experienced, having shorter file checkpoints could end up in increased disk usage.

We first investigate this phenomenon in depth in this notebook: [ttft_investigations_notebook.ipynb](./ttft_investigations_notebook.ipynb) (note that this is a living notebook, and we hope to keep it up-to-date with our findings as they evolve).

At a high-level, we find that among widely-used tokenizers, the `AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained` interface of `transformers` and the `CTokenizer` interface of `datasets` (the `PreTrainedTokenizerFast` and `SimpleTokenizer` interfaces respectively of `datasets` have the desired behavior out of the box) replace certain terms with `<unk>` false-positively.

We recommend the `PreTrainedTokenizerFast` and `SimpleTokenizer` interfaces for tokenization of files to make sure you do not run into the `<unk>` issue, or otherwise manually construct vocab files in a way as to remove all terms with one-letter titles (for example, by using the interface of `tokenizers` which does not replace invalid terms).

Additionally, we can defer loading the vocab files until the very end of file processing, and only load them for generating `bytes` using vocab file. However, the vocab loading process itself is quite costly, and this approach can increase file processing time significantly, defeat the purpose of making file processing parallel, and could end up being more costly as well.


