[170/200] Running: How does the tokenizer handle emojis and symbols?...

🧠 LLM Response:

---------------------------------------------------

To tokenize emojis and symbols you can use A regex tokenizer and add the
emoji and Unicode Scalar groups to your pattern.

For example, using Python and the `re` (regex) tokenizer you can tokenize
emojis this way:

```
import re

emojis = re.compile(
          '(?:' + re.escape('💃') + '|' + re.escape('😀') + '|'  # Emojis
          '|['    # Symbols
          u'\u2000-\u3300'  # General Punctuation
          u'\u2100-\u276F'  # Letterlike Symbols
          u'\u2700-\u4fff'  # Dingbats
          u'\u23f2-\uf0ff'  # Mathematical Operators
          ']+)'
)

def parse(text):
  return emojis.split(text)

parse('😍😀✊')
"""
['', '😍', '😀', '✊', '']
"""
```

Here, `re.compile` builds the regular expression to match the
characters defined by the pattern. The `re.escape` makes sure to correctly
escape the given sequences (although in this case unnecessary for the
emojis).

You can adapt this regex to cover other Unicode types of characters you
want to match. Note this will fail to properly handle emoji strings with
spaces, so you may need a more sophisticated parser if dealing with such
text. Also note you should install a new font with those emojis if you
want them to be displayed correctly on your browser.

**Sources**:

-   [Emojipedia on Unicode
    standard](https://emojipedia.org/unicode-emoji/)
-   [Regular expression for
    emojis](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/34016977/regular-expression-for-emoticons-in-social-network-posts-excluding-shortcuts)
