🧠 LLM Response:
 Whether you’re running at the edge or in the cloud, inference latency is a critical metric for deploying models. To optimize inference speed, a standard rule of thumb is to balance model complexity and accuracy: smaller models tend to yield lower inference latency at the cost of model expressivity, whereas larger models provide more nuanced outputs but require more computational resources. By comparing model performance across a spectrum of different sizes, we can form an understanding of the key factors that impact model inference runtime.

In this blog post, we investigate the relationship between model size and inference latency for a diverse set of generative models: Generative Flow (Glow)^{[1]}, Variational Autoencoder (VAE)^{[2]}, Gated Recurrent Unit VAE (GRU-VAE)^{[3]}, and autoregressive models with Transformer (AT) blocks and dilated convolutions (WaveNet & Sparse Transformer)^{[4][5]}^{[6]}. For Glow, we create models of varying complexity by tuning the number of depth scaling hyperparameter `L`. In the case of VAEs, models of increasing capacities are constructed by modulating the architecture of the CVAE encoder and decoder with various width and depth settings. Sparse Transformer is a deep autoregressive model, so we explore model sizes by adjusting the number of Transformer layers. To contrast, we include WaveNet, which is relatively shallow but has a wider receptive field.

We run inference experiments on two accelerators: a 2.6 GHz Intel Xeon CPU (CPU) and NVIDIA P100 GPU (GPU). We also study the effect of dilation cycles on both the WaveNet and Sparse Transformer inferences, which are periodic functions that alternate between sub-steps and full-steps. Our primary goal is to profile the inference latency as a function of the model parameters. Since we’re interested in evaluating the effect that model sizes have on inference speed, for every experiment in this study, we ensure that all models are configured to match the desired bitrate of 44.1 kHz.

Before proceeding, let’s start by defining the key terms used in our study. Model size refers to the total number of unique model parameters required to represent the architecture. For non-generative models, this equates to the number of parameters representing the full model. In contrast, for a stochastic generative model, the model size only includes the architecture representing the entire generative process; the sampling/inference step is excluded without affecting the model size. Inference latency measures the total amount of time it takes to execute a single model forward-pass through a generative model, returned as an integer number of samples. Later, we refer to the number of samples per second (Samp/s) as the inference speed , evaluated by taking the reciprocal of inference latency. The two most relevant factors affecting inference latency are (1) the number of model operations executed during the forward-pass, quantified as the number of floating point operations (FLOPs), and (2) the number of end-to-end model computations occurring in parallel.

To reliably estimate the true inference latency, we repeat every experiment 500 times and report the median results (25th and 75th percentile ranges included as error bars). The curves presented in the following plots are intended to demonstrate latency trends, and not to be used as exact inference latency measurements. This is to say, future inference tests may produce different curves that share the same fundamental trends presented in our study.

# Glow

We first analyze the results of our Glow model. We conduct experiments using three model variants: L=1, L=3, and L=6. Note that, as the value of L increases, the model complexity grows. All three models are configured to produce 44.1 kHz Mel-spectrogram outputs.

We start by profiling the inference latency of every Glow variant on the CPU (Figure 1, left) and GPU (Figure 1, right). For both platforms, the inference latency decreases with increased `L` (i.e., as the model size grows), meaning the inference speed increases. When comparing the two processing units, we see that the inference latency is generally lower on the GPU than the CPU. This is an expected result; the GPU has higher parallelism and gigaflops-per-watt efficiency than the CPU, which benefits the high computational requirements associated with running large neural network
