So the idea here would be, if you're exposing your training jobs via some gateway service - whether that's a REST API or a MQTT interface, for example - you should measure the characteristics of the network latency from the client talking to the service. And then to the gateway (the service itself), and then from the gateway to the server. So you have three pieces of the whole latency picture.
Now, at each of those latencies, you want to look at what the distribution looks like. Are there standard deviations you can measure? Even if you don't know the underlying distribution (and usually you don't), you can measure these standard deviations and build them into your service level agreements, or your requirements around both latency and throughput.
The network latency from the client to the service is a fixed value that you measure. Let's say 5ms. Plus the gateway service's handling time, which we'll also capture at 10ms, for a total handling latency between the client and service combination of 15ms. Then you have the network delay from the service to the server, which is also fixed and we can measure the standard deviation or other distribution variations. Finally, the server's handling time independent of the operations running on it should be 5ms.
Then you add up these network latencies and subtract them from whatever batch size you've chosen to conduct your operations. And the result is what you might get as the first glimpse of the throughput in your network. Obviously it's not perfect, but it get's you close, and allows you to estimate both latency and throughput at least in the first cut.
There's more detail to all of this, but that's a high level overview. I hope it gets you started on the right path.

